## Errors & Mistakes
The first documentation-generation run during the final audit could not start the locally built provider inside the process sandbox and reported "Unrecognized remote plugin message". The same command passed with normal process permissions. The first public commit metadata included local validation paths and research details that were not part of the public contract; the commit message and notes reference were rewritten during the public audit. The first live acceptance test was in the root Go package, which the repository acceptance matrix does not schedule. The final CI audit found this gap, so the data source and tests moved into a caller_identity package and that suite was added to both acceptance-test lists. The first public CI rerun also repeated unrelated failures in CKS cleanup, inference prerequisites, and object-storage convergence. The initial public commit and pull request described the data source without citing the AWS interface that inspired it; both were amended when the user identified the omission. The first attempt to format the documentation links used Markdown backticks inside a Go raw string, which caused "syntax error: unexpected name aws_caller_identity after top level declaration". Removing code formatting from the link labels fixed the source, and documentation generation then passed.

## Dead Ends
No existing provider dependency exposes this identity route. Adding a new schema dependency was unnecessary for a two-field read, so the implementation uses the authenticated JSON endpoint and the provider's existing HTTP client. Leaving the live test in the root package would have made it manual-only, so the data source uses a suite-shaped package even though it has no resources to sweep. Additional identity attributes were excluded because the requested public contract contains only organization_id and user_id. The cloud-provider implementations were used as public interface references, not copied, because CoreWeave has its own API and client conventions. Google does not expose one exact account-and-caller equivalent: google_client_openid_userinfo describes the authenticated account, while google_client_config describes configured project context. AzureAD client config was also examined, but AzureRM client config was selected for the public cross-cloud comparison because it is the direct counterpart in the Azure infrastructure provider.

## Hints for Future Agents
The JSON response maps principal.orgUid to organization_id and principal.uid to user_id. Connect interceptors do not apply to the direct JSON request, so the request must attach the configured bearer token and user agent. Keep the schema at two computed attributes unless the public product contract expands. Update both the make suite list and workflow suite list when renaming or removing the acceptance package. Preserve the cross-cloud comparison in public metadata and generated documentation, but do not claim identical semantics: AWS exposes account and caller IDs, AzureRM exposes configured client, tenant, subscription, and object IDs, and Google OpenID userinfo exposes the authenticated account email.

## Codebase Discoveries
Data sources are registered by the provider's DataSources method. Documentation is generated from examples and provider schemas with make generate. A schema MarkdownDescription is rendered into both the data-source page body and its frontmatter description. The shared retryable HTTP client supplies the existing timeout and retry behavior to the identity request. Acceptance suites are selected independently by GNUmakefile and the pull request workflow; root-package acceptance tests are not included. The AWS caller identity data source calls STS GetCallerIdentity. AzureRM client config reads client, tenant, subscription, and object IDs from provider state. Google client OpenID userinfo calls the OpenID userinfo endpoint and requires the userinfo.email scope.

